1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,691
Who creates your Java objects?

2
00:00:01,891 --> 00:00:03,060
Without Spring, your code

3
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:04,880
creates a repository,

4
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,907
then passes it into a service.

5
00:00:07,147 --> 00:00:10,080
With Spring, you register bean definitions.

6
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,660
The container uses them to create

7
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:12,620
managed objects

8
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:15,100
and wire their dependencies.

9
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:18,320
Here, the service bean asks for a repository.

10
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:19,923
Spring supplies that bean.

11
00:00:20,163 --> 00:00:21,860
Your application calls getBean

12
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:24,180
to request the ready service.

13
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:25,700
In our Java run, its repository

14
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:28,991
is the same bean from the container.

15
00:00:29,251 --> 00:00:31,580
Your app starts Spring with Config.

16
00:00:31,580 --> 00:00:33,180
Spring calls Config’s bean methods,

17
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:34,020
creates Repo,

18
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:35,920
and wires Service.

19
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:37,940
Your app retrieves the ready service.

20
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:39,963
Managed beans only.

21
00:00:41,963 --> 00:00:43,053
Thanks for watching.

